//
// Expected: a1 at one session and at two, and the same codes as
// order_relay_declared_later.vp, which differs only in where Hop's first
// block sits.
//
// At one session the attacker bypasses Relay: it replaces body at Hop with
// nil, so Hop and Relay forward nil, and hands Bob the body it observed on
// Alice's send. Bob's ASSERT passes on a body Relay never sent. The judge
// once excused a delivered value equal to the honest one when the sender
// had not sent it, which pinned a0 at one session.
// body = MAC(k, label) is the same in every session. At two sessions the
// attacker replaces body at Hop with nil, so Relay forwards nil and Bob's
// check would halt him, and delivers Bob the body Relay#2 sends in its place:
// Relay sent it once and Bob and Bob#2 both accept it. The executor once ran
// principals in declaration order and let an install through before its
// sender had sent the same value, which then counted as attacker-authored.
// Declaring Hop first let the attacker deliver Alice's own body to Hop before
// Alice sent it, marking every forwarded copy authored, so Relay's emission
// stopped counting and the verdict followed the declaration order. A receive
// now waits for its sender unless the phase can make no other progress.
attacker[active]

principal Hop[
	knows public label
]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public label
	body = MAC(k, label)
]

Alice -> Hop: body

principal Hop[
	_ = HASH(body)
]

Hop -> Relay: body

principal Relay[
	_ = HASH(body)
]

Relay -> Bob: body

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	knows public label
	_ = ASSERT(body, MAC(k, label))?
]

queries[
	authentication? Relay -> Bob: body
]
